  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 218 AM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nThe boundary that moved through Wednesday has stalled to our south \nand is evident from southwestern Missouri west-northwest into \nnorthwestern Kansas. As high pressure moves southeastward into the \nGreat Lakes, weakening as it does so, low-level flow will increase \nfrom the south across Kansas and to a lesser extent western \nMissouri. This will lift that stalled boundary back northward \nthrough the day so that by around 21Z, it will be situated from\neastern Nebraska southeast into central Missouri. Moisture may \npool again along this boundary as it is lifting northward and \nwith temperatures likely to climb into the low to mid 80s again,\ndewpoints may climb into lower 60s. So it will once again feel \nhot for anyone outside this afternoon. Our normal high \ntemperatures today range from the upper 60s, in northeastern MO,\nto near 70 degrees, in the Kansas City area. So we'll once \nagain be looking at highs at least 10-15 degrees above normal, \nwith some areas pushing 20 degrees above normal. \n\nAs has been the case for a few nights now, models show an area \nof ascent developing as the low-level jet strengthens tonight \ninto Friday morning. This ascent is fairly deep, evident from \n300K to 310K. So showers storms seem likely for areas to our \nnorth. Forecast soundings for northern and northeastern Missouri\nshow deep dry air below 10000 to 12000 feet. With the strongest\nascent north of the area and deep dry air to overcome, it's \nvery unlikely anything develops or moves into our northern zones\ntonight. So the forecast remains dry with only ~5% PoPs for our\nfar northern zones. \n\nThe forecast through Monday continues to look well above normal \nWe're very likely to see highs continue to be 10-15 degrees above \nnormal, with some areas, mainly in our southern and southeastern \nzones, pushing 20 degrees above normal. High temperatures will \nbe pushing the lower 90s in our southern and southeastern zones \ngiven the dominant upper ridging across the middle of the \ncountry. Within this ridging though, the remnants of Isaias will\npass to our east, aided by a strong shortwave moving through \nthe Southern Plains. The upper ridge reestablishes itself and \namplifies across the middle of the country through the middle of\nthe week. Ensemble mean 500mb heights are greater than the 90th\npercentile. If this verifies, our highs will be right back in \nthe mid to upper 80s, and pushing 90 degrees, well above our \ncurrent forecast. Forecast uncertainty is very high and the \nspread between the 25th and 75th percentiles is around 15 \ndegrees across the area. This increase to greater than 20 \ndegrees comparing the 10 and 90th percentiles. This uncertainty \nnaturally spills into the PoP forecast as well. If the upper \nridging is stronger, like the deterministic GFS shows, and the \nensemble 500mb mean indicates, the chances for precipitation \ndecrease substantially for Tuesday into Wednesday. If we see \nsomething more in line with the deterministic Canadian, which \nhas a shortwave moving through, or the deterministic ECMWF, \nwhich has the ridge further east, we would have better chances \nfor rain.",
